INSERT INTO city_chunks VALUES(29,'atlanta',2,'nt PIT data show a 22% decrease in adults with children experiencing homelessness since earlier years. In 2024, there were 115 homeless families, with most in emergency shelters and a few unsheltered. - FACT: Long-term rehousing progress - City: Atlanta - Topic: housing_exits_trend - Content: Since 2016, Atlanta has housed over 12,500 people, including more than 2,000 households in 2023 alone. That year, 800 formerly unsheltered households were housed through rehousing efforts. ### Demographic patterns - FACT: Gender patterns - Topic: demographics_gender - Content: Men and boys make up about 73% of people experiencing homelessness in Atlanta, while women and girls are about 25%. Around 2% identify as transgender, non‑binary, multiple genders, or culturally specific identities. - FACT: Age patterns - Topic: demographics_age - Content: About 35% of adults experiencing homelessness in Atlanta are over age 55. Youth and young adults (18–24) form a distinct group that has recently increased and needs tailored services. - FACT: Race and ethnicity – overall - Topic: demographics_race_overall - Content: About 86% of people experiencing homelessness in Atlanta are Black or African American. This pattern also appears among families, youth, and veterans, pointing to systemic racial disparities in who becomes homeless.','[0.0220489501953125,-0.0226287841796875,0.035614013671875,-0.0086669921875,0.060699462890625,0.035400390625,0.0916748046875,0.0662841796875,-0.03411865234375,-0.01898193359375,-0.064697265625,-0.04339599609375,-0.07177734375,0.0202484130859375,-0.03350830078125,0.047760009765625,0.06561279296875,-0.033050537109375,0.0231170654296875,0.0033321380615234375,0.0265655517578125,0.020294189453125,-0.0158538818359375,0.027435302734375,0.0584716796875,-0.026123046875,0.0116119384765625,-0.037109375,-0.054901123046875,-0.00725555419921875,0.003498077392578125,-0.02899169921875,0.008148193359375,-0.0283966064453125,0.0328369