INSERT INTO city_chunks VALUES(30,'atlanta',3,'ople experiencing homelessness in Atlanta are Black or African American. This pattern also appears among families, youth, and veterans, pointing to systemic racial disparities in who becomes homeless. - FACT: Race and ethnicity – families, youth, veterans - Topic: demographics_race_subgroups - Content: Among homeless families, 92% are Black. About 77% of homeless youth are Black, with some identifying as multiracial, and around 83% of homeless veterans are Black, with smaller shares White, multiracial, or other races. - FACT: Women and girls in families - Topic: demographics_gender_families - Content: Within homeless families, women and girls make up about 62%, a higher share than in the general homeless population. Reports from families show no adults identifying outside cisgender categories in this subgroup. - FACT: Youth gender diversity - Topic: demographics_gender_youth - Content: Among homeless youth, about 60% are men or boys and 35% women or girls. Around 4% identify as transgender and 2% as non‑binary or multigender, showing more gender diversity than among older adults. - FACT: Veterans and gender - Topic: demographics_gender_veterans - Content: Men make up about 91% of homeless veterans in the Atlanta CoC. Women represent about 8%, and around 1% identify with a culturally specific gender identity.','[0.043243408203125,-0.00020623207092285156,-0.005039215087890625,0.0006594657897949219,0.06353759765625,0.06817626953125,0.0679931640625,0.03863525390625,-0.0396728515625,-0.02362060546875,-0.07733154296875,0.004207611083984375,-0.07177734375,0.00637054443359375,-0.05670166015625,0.055389404296875,0.07440185546875,-0.00640106201171875,0.040802001953125,-0.016876220703125,0.0261688232421875,-0.0018892288208007812,-0.0157318115234375,0.035125732421875,0.05316162109375,-0.0137786865234375,0.0265045166015625,-0.0252685546875,-0.052490234375,-0.0201568603515625,0.00067901611328125,-0.038330078125,-0.0032520294189453125