INSERT INTO city_chunks VALUES(41,'boston',2,'two years, more than 20,000 migrants, many from Haiti and Central/South America and some fleeing gang violence, have arrived in MA. Many depend on shelters or unstable, overcrowded, or unsafe housing. ### Contributing factors (city- and region-specific) - FACT: Housing market pressures - Topic: contributing_factors_housing - Content: Greater Boston’s crisis reflects high rents, restrictive zoning, and costly development. MA was short nearly 200,000 low‑income homes by 2021, and shelters were already full. Two‑bedrooms in nearby cities can exceed $2,400. - FACT: Employment and wages - Topic: contributing_factors_employment - Content: Even with steady full‑time work, many cannot meet high rent, income, or occupancy requirements. Migrant and low‑income families are often rejected by landlords or pushed into shelters, doubling up, or cars and abandoned spaces. - FACT: Policy environment (Right-to-Shelter and time limits) - Topic: contributing_factors_policy - Content: MA’s Right‑to‑Shelter for families is now capped at 7,500 families and limited to roughly 9‑month stays in state shelters. Providers warn these limits, in a tight market, will push some families into unsheltered or street homelessness.','[-0.012603759765625,-0.040313720703125,0.0298614501953125,-0.040496826171875,0.05633544921875,0.03509521484375,0.039337158203125,0.0386962890625,-0.0233612060546875,-0.027435302734375,-0.046539306640625,-0.044677734375,-0.0638427734375,0.048919677734375,-0.033843994140625,0.05523681640625,0.05108642578125,-0.042022705078125,0.00012695789337158203,-0.02142333984375,0.037017822265625,-0.0161895751953125,0.0462646484375,0.0301666259765625,0.01114654541015625,-0.031524658203125,0.00217437744140625,-0.0239410400390625,-0.06134033203125,-0.021881103515625,0.015472412109375,0.002262115478515625,-0.027008056640625,-0.040679931640625,0.03265380859375,0.003383636474609375,-0.023101806640625,0.0205230712890625,0.010009765625,-0.0170135498