INSERT INTO city_chunks VALUES(42,'boston',3,'is now capped at 7,500 families and limited to roughly 9‑month stays in state shelters. Providers warn these limits, in a tight market, will push some families into unsheltered or street homelessness. --- ## Life paths into homelessness and barriers ### Common life paths (general) - PATHWAY: Escaping domestic or sexual violence - Applies to: Adult survivors of domestic violence and sexual assault - Content: Some adults become homeless after fleeing domestic or sexual violence. Boston tracks “adult survivors of domestic violence” as a subpopulation, whose counts fell from 385 in 2024 to 174 in 2025, closer to earlier years. - PATHWAY: Family and child homelessness - Applies to: Families with children in Greater Boston - Content: Families and children drive much of Greater Boston’s increase. Between 2023 and 2024, people in families made up nearly all new homelessness, and youth under 18 saw especially large rate increases tied to housing instability. - PATHWAY: Migrants fleeing violence and instability - Applies to: Newly arrived migrants - Content: Many migrants flee gang violence, political instability, or economic collapse and then face MA’s severe housing shortage. They confront sky‑high rents, few vacancies, language barriers, and limited access to permanent affordable units.','[-0.01541900634765625,-0.02203369140625,0.02850341796875,-0.03497314453125,0.06585693359375,0.0160980224609375,0.067626953125,0.041107177734375,-0.043548583984375,-0.03533935546875,-0.06683349609375,-0.0298309326171875,-0.0550537109375,0.0187225341796875,0.0001480579376220703,0.04327392578125,0.06793212890625,-0.01190185546875,-0.01300048828125,-0.0257110595703125,0.037078857421875,0.01462554931640625,0.01403045654296875,0.060272216796875,0.033477783203125,-0.0174560546875,0.00913238525390625,-0.01216888427734375,-0.07757568359375,-0.0033283233642578125,0.0222015380859375,-0.0272216796875,-0.0190887451171875,-0.01548004150390625,0.033477783203