INSERT INTO city_chunks VALUES(46,'boston',7,'4–2025, adults in emergency shelters rose 1,544→1,648 as Boston added beds. New programs for men and women aimed to deconcentrate large shelters and spread clients across multiple, less crowded sites. - FACT: Shelter and transitional housing for families - Topic: services_shelter_families - Content: In Boston, people in families in emergency shelter fell 3,670→3,365 and family households 1,125→1,081 from 2024–2025. People in families in transitional housing dropped from 50 to 19, indicating a smaller transitional stock. - FACT: Veterans - Topic: subpopulation_veterans - Content: Homeless veterans in Boston fell from 228 to about 194 between 2024–2025, with very few unsheltered veterans. Since 2011, veteran homelessness has dropped by more than half due to sustained strategies and partnerships. - FACT: Unaccompanied youth - Topic: subpopulation_youth - Content: Unaccompanied youth (up to age 24) decreased slightly in Boston, from 132 to 128 between 2024–2025. Nearly all were in shelters or programs; only one unsheltered youth was identified in the 2025 street count. - FACT: Exits to permanent housing - Topic: housing_exits - Content: In 2024, 2,838 people exited homelessness to permanent housing in Boston, up from 2,019 in 2023. These exits included adult individuals, 545 families, unaccompanied youth, and 174 veterans, showing expanded housing placements.','[0.007495880126953125,-0.0364990234375,0.0209197998046875,-0.030731201171875,0.049591064453125,-0.00775909423828125,0.0589599609375,0.043060302734375,-0.044647216796875,-0.028106689453125,-0.08331298828125,-0.032012939453125,-0.0455322265625,0.003261566162109375,-0.00797271728515625,0.048095703125,0.07427978515625,-0.030548095703125,0.0247650146484375,0.0019359588623046875,0.049591064453125,-0.007335662841796875,0.0011157989501953125,0.03338623046875,0.04229736328125,-0.0220184326171875,0.02630615234375,-0.038787841796875,-0.053253173828125,-0.0227203369140625,-0.0072