INSERT INTO city_chunks VALUES(62,'la',7,'rm responses alone have not fully addressed root causes such as housing costs, income inequality, and behavioral health gaps. Sustained, comprehensive strategies are needed to change long‑term trends. - FACT: Rent and homelessness link (national research) - Topic: contributing_factors_rent_elasticity - Content: National research cited for LA indicates that each $100 rise in median rent is linked to roughly a 9% increase in estimated homelessness rates. LA’s high and rising rents amplify this effect locally. --- ## Subpopulations and specific groups - FACT: Families - City: Los Angeles CoC - Topic: subpopulation_families - Content: From 2023–2024, people in families experiencing homelessness increased from about 10,477 to 10,710 (≈2.2%). Families remain a significant share, even amid some system gains. - FACT: Transitional age youth - City: Los Angeles CoC - Topic: subpopulation_youth - Content: Transitional age youth counts fell from about 2,871 to 2,406 (≈16% drop) between 2023–2024. This suggests some progress but youth homelessness remains a priority focus. - FACT: Veterans - City: Los Angeles CoC - Topic: subpopulation_veterans - Content: Veterans experiencing homelessness decreased from roughly 3,878 to 2,991 (≈23% drop). This indicates improved housing outcomes for veterans compared to some other groups.','[0.03021240234375,-0.0341796875,0.037384033203125,-0.002109527587890625,0.08477783203125,0.0188140869140625,0.05474853515625,0.052093505859375,-0.024810791015625,-0.07568359375,-0.0650634765625,-0.014617919921875,-0.041717529296875,0.031463623046875,-0.00077056884765625,0.06298828125,0.05572509765625,-0.0257415771484375,0.02667236328125,-0.00902557373046875,-0.00736236572265625,0.0227203369140625,0.0012912750244140625,0.03118896484375,0.0235595703125,-0.044036865234375,0.0120697021484375,-0.0199127197265625,-0.08148193359375,-0.00852203369140625,0.00858306884765625,0.005565643310546875,-0.02532958984375,-0.0136795043