INSERT INTO city_chunks VALUES(65,'nyc',0,'# City: New York, NY # Scope: Homelessness context and resources # Timeframe: Data primarily from 2020–2025 (city and statewide context) ## Summary (High-level) - Between January 2022 and January 2024, homelessness in New York State more than doubled; from 2023–2024 it rose 53%, far faster than the U.S. overall. - In 2024, over 158,000 New Yorkers were homeless (about 1 in 5 of all U.S. homeless people), with New York City driving most of the increase and a very large share of children. - New York has extremely low unsheltered rates due to its right-to-shelter and extensive emergency beds, but faces severe pressures from high rents, evictions, asylum-seeker arrivals, and housing shortages. --- ## Overall statistics and trends ### Population level and trends (state + city) - FACT: Statewide growth in homelessness - City: New York (Statewide context) - Topic: population_trend - Content: Statewide homelessness more than doubled between January 2022 and January 2024. From 2023–2024 alone, it grew 53%, compared with about 21% growth in the rest of the nation.','[0.0084075927734375,-0.057281494140625,0.03338623046875,-0.0019893646240234375,0.08392333984375,0.024322509765625,0.06396484375,0.0474853515625,-0.02264404296875,-0.0406494140625,-0.03167724609375,-0.053070068359375,-0.054046630859375,0.023101806640625,-0.0012197494506835938,0.051666259765625,0.06048583984375,-0.0023937225341796875,0.004573822021484375,0.005405426025390625,0.00437164306640625,-0.0003705024719238281,0.0254058837890625,0.01441192626953125,0.0149993896484375,-0.0263214111328125,0.0014667510986328125,-0.04400634765625,-0.0712890625,-0.017852783203125,-0.007091522216796875,-0.023651123046875,-0.0223388671875,-0.02154541015625,0.058319091796875,0.0256805419921875,-0.0269012451171875,0.004650115966796875,0.014373779296875,-0.00589752197265625,-0.0257415771484375,-0.03399658203125,-0.0278778076171875,0.0176239013671875,-0.064453125,-0.006320953369140625,0.0425109