INSERT INTO city_chunks VALUES(66,'nyc',1,'population_trend - Content: Statewide homelessness more than doubled between January 2022 and January 2024. From 2023–2024 alone, it grew 53%, compared with about 21% growth in the rest of the nation. - FACT: National share and rate - City: New York (Statewide) - Topic: population_national_share - Content: In 2024, more than 158,000 people in New York experienced homelessness, about 1 in 5 of all people homeless in the U.S. The state’s rate was ~8 per 1,000 residents, among the highest nationally. - FACT: New York City’s role in statewide trends - City: New York City - Topic: population_trend_nyc - Content: New York City accounted for about 93–95% of the statewide increase. People in DHS shelters grew from 45,343 in January 2022 to 89,119 in January 2024, including over 34,000 asylum seekers. - FACT: NYC scale (recent peak) - City: New York City - Topic: population_recent_peak - Content: In November 2025, about 101,978 people slept in NYC shelters nightly, the highest since the Great Depression. Thousands more were unsheltered or doubled up, bringing the total without stable housing to an estimated 350,000+. - FACT: Children and families (statewide) - City: New York (Statewide) - Topic: population_children_state - Content: Homeless children increased from about 20,299 in 2022 to 50,773 in 2024.','[-0.003711700439453125,-0.060394287109375,0.041839599609375,0.0035343170166015625,0.089111328125,0.00954437255859375,0.058380126953125,0.054046630859375,-0.032867431640625,-0.0296478271484375,-0.0242767333984375,-0.032745361328125,-0.070068359375,0.036468505859375,0.01087188720703125,0.049102783203125,0.07244873046875,0.005435943603515625,0.016143798828125,0.013214111328125,-0.0023746490478515625,-0.0179595947265625,0.0187225341796875,-0.00182342529296875,0.0198516845703125,-0.0400390625,0.01459503173828125,-0.044952392578125,-0.0628662109375,-0.0237274169921875,-0.01137542724609375,-0.0189666748046875,-0.032012939453125,0.01469421